ue,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":10,"renderedHeight":10,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":232},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_out.png","alt":"External Web Site Icon","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":10,"renderedHeight":10,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":232},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_out.png","alt":"External Web Site Icon","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":10,"renderedHeight":10,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":232},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_out.png","alt":"External Web Site Icon","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":10,"renderedHeight":10,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":232},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_pdf.gif","alt":"Adobe PDF file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":6058},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_out.png","alt":"External Web Site Icon","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":10,"renderedHeight":10,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":232},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/r6404f1.gif","alt":"The figure shows the formation and activity of the main anthrax toxins. Bacillus anthracis has three major virulence factors: an antiphagocytic capsule and two exotoxins, edema toxin (ET) and lethal toxin (LT). Much of the morbidity and mortality observed with anthrax is attributed to the enzymatic effects of these toxins. Protective antigen (PA) combines with edema factor (EF) and lethal factor (LF) to form binary combinations of ET and LT.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":834,"renderedHeight":560,"naturalWidth":834,"naturalHeight":560,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 445 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":89024},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/r6404f2.gif","alt":"The figure shows a clinical algorithm that might assist with anthrax mass-casualty incident planning. The clinical algorithm addresses four decision points where resource limitations might impact clinical management during an anthrax mass-casualty incident. The first decision point involves diagnostic evaluation of anthrax meningitis, which is necessary to determine optimal antimicrobial therapy. The second decision point involves treatment regimens of combination intravenous antimicrobial therapy. The third decision point involves administration of antitoxins, as an adjunctive therapy. The fourth decision point involves the identification and drainage of fluid collections, which have been associated with a survival benefit. Local triage teams should be activated as outlined in the IOM 2012 Crisis Standards of Care. Admission criteria for nonsystemically ill patients may depend on local and regional bed space. Clinicians may choose to administer a second oral antimicrobial for patients with nonspecific symptoms of anthrax who do not meet criteria for hospitalization. Close follow-up is recommended. Patients with meningitis or in whom meningitis cannot be excluded should be treated aggressively and receive antitoxin even if an antitoxin shortage exists. Drainage of effusions improves survival. Clinicians experienced with management of effusions should assess for significant fluid collections and drain as possible under the current standard of care (see Box 6). Patients with meningitis or in whom meningitis cannot be excluded should receive a minimum of 14 days of IV antimicrobials before stepping down to single agent.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":834,"renderedHeight":979,"naturalWidth":834,"naturalHeight":979,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 1644 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":58953},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/r6404f3.gif","alt":"The figure shows the treatment regimen for systemic anthrax with suspected, possible, or confirmed meningitis. Preferred drugs are indicated in boldface. Alternative drugs are listed in order of preference for treatment for patients who cannot take first-line treatment or if first-line treatment is unavailable. Rifampin is not a protein synthesis inhibitor. However, it may be used in combination with other antimicrobials drugs on the basis of its in vitro synergy.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":833,"renderedHeight":563,"naturalWidth":833,"naturalHeight":563,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 468 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":22628},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_out.png","alt":"External Web Site Icon","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":10,"renderedHeight":10,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":232},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/r6404f4.gif","alt":"The figure shows the treatment regimen for systemic anthrax when meningitis has been excluded. Ciprofloxacin is the preferred bactericidal agent, and clindamycin is the preferred protein synthesis inhibitor. Alternative drugs are listed in order of preference for treatment for patients who cannot take first-line treatment or if first-line treatment is unavailable. Ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin, moxifloxacin, meropenem, imipenem, doripenem, penicillin G, ampicillin, clindamycin, linezolid, and rifampin are antimicrobials with good central nervous system penetration. Rifampin is not a protein synthesis inhibitor. However, it may be used in combination with other antimicrobials drugs on the basis of its in vitro synergy.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":837,"renderedHeight":505,"naturalWidth":837,"naturalHeight":505,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 725 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":23145},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_out.png","alt":"External Web Site Icon","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":10,"renderedHeight":10,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":232},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_pdf.gif","alt":"Adobe PDF file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,